<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="chapter4" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Solution Sets</title>

  <introduction>

<p>
    In this chapter, we perform a deep dive into solution sets of matrix equations <m>A\vect{x}=\vect{b}</m>.  We reconceptualize solution sets as intersections of hyperplanes and deduce that solution sets are <m>k</m>-planes themselves.  We then go on to use this perspective to construct an algorithmic strategy for finding the parametric vector form of solution sets.
</p>

<p>
    <alert> <xref ref="chp4sec1" />.</alert>
    Matrix equations arise in many quantitative situations and we frequently want to understand its solution set.  
    If we reconceptualize a matrix equation <m>A\vect{x}=\vect{b}</m> as a system of hyperplanes, then we may conceptualize its solution set <m>\Sol(A,\vect{b})</m> as the intersection of these hyperplanes.  
    This geometric perspective unlocks new strategies of analyzing solution sets.
</p>

<objectives>
    <title>Objectives for <xref ref="chp4sec1" /></title>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>I can <alert>interpret</alert> a matrix equation as a collection of hyperplanes.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>I can <alert>interpret</alert> a solution set as an intersection of hyperplanes.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>I can <alert>visualize</alert> configurations of hyperplanes and their intersections in <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m>, <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m>.</p>
        </li>
    </ul>
</objectives>

<p>
    <alert> <xref ref="chp4sec2" />.</alert>  
    Hyperplanes can form many different types of configurations, and hence so too can their intersections.  
    We explore these different possible types of intersections of hyperplanes and construct expectations for their likelihoods.
</p>

<objectives>
    <title>Objectives for <xref ref="chp4sec2" /></title>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>I can <alert>verbalize</alert> and <alert>visualize</alert> the different types of configurations of hyperplanes in <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m> and <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m>. </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>I can <alert>justify</alert> my expectations for intersections of random hyperplanes in <m>\mathbb{R}^n</m>.</p>
        </li>
    </ul>
</objectives>

<p>
    <alert> <xref ref="chp4sec3a" />.</alert>  The intersection of hyperplanes is a <m>k</m>-plane for some <m>k</m>.  
    Having explored and developed our conceptualization of solution sets as intersections, we now leverage this understanding to compute the parametric vector form of solution sets.  
    In so doing, we introduce several new actions and objects:   elementary row operations, reduced row echelon form, and Gaussian elimination.
</p>

<objectives>
    <title>Objectives for <xref ref="chp4sec3a" /></title>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>I can <alert>explain</alert> the goal of elimination and how it relates to solution sets of matrix equations.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>I can <alert>justify</alert> why row operations do not change the system's intersection.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>I can <alert>symbolize</alert> a matrix equation as an augmented matrix.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>I can <alert>symbolize</alert> row operations.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>I can <alert>compute</alert> by hand the reduced row echelon form (rref) of small matrices.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>I can use technology to <alert>compute</alert> the reduced row echelon form of large matrices.</p>
        </li>
    </ul>
</objectives>

<p>
    <alert> <xref ref="chp4sec3b" />.</alert>  We analyze rref and interpret its meanings in the context of solution sets.
</p>

<objectives>
    <title>Objectives for <xref ref="chp4sec3b" /></title>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>I can <alert>interpret</alert> the reduced row echelon form of an augmented matrix to write its solution set in parametric vector form.</p>
        </li>
    </ul>
</objectives>

<p>
    <alert> <xref ref="chp4sec4" />.</alert>  
    The three elementary row operations can be encoded by multiplication by elementary matrices.  These elementary matrices are familiar geometric transformations: reflections, stretches, and shears.  You should practice moving between row operations their associated elementary matrices.  We may then encode sequences of row operations with products of elementary matrices.
</p>

<objectives>
    <title>Objectives for <xref ref="chp4sec4" /></title>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>I understand which row operations correspond to which elementary matrices. </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>I can <alert>interpret</alert> a sequence of row operations as a product of elementary matrices.</p>
        </li>
    </ul>
</objectives>

  </introduction>

